+*   **When to use:** Direct volume rendering of participating media where scattering is negligible or approximated by attenuation [S3, unofficial].
 *   **When not to use:** When realistic light transport involving multiple bounces or refraction is required [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Emission-Absorption Model is derived from the physical model of light transport in participating media [S3]. It is based on the volume rendering equation, first formalized by Max [S1]. The specific simplification where emission is proportional to density (Sabella's density emitter model) was introduced by Sabella [S4, S5]. It is considered "Low Albedo Volume Rendering" because it assumes low reflectivity (albedo), ignoring multiple scattering events [S4]. It is the standard model for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) [S3].
+The Emission-Absorption Model is derived from the physical model of light transport in participating media [S3, unofficial]. It is based on the volume rendering equation, first formalized by Max [S1]. The specific simplification where emission is proportional to density (Sabella's density emitter model) was introduced by Sabella [S4, S5]. It is considered "Low Albedo Volume Rendering" because it assumes low reflectivity (albedo), ignoring multiple scattering events [S4]. It is the standard model for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) [S3, unofficial].
 
